<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Syr Darya</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Syr Darya is a major river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> that flows from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a> mountains north-westwards to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aral-sea">Aral Sea</a>. Known to classical writers as the Jaxartes, it marked an important frontier between the settled lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> and the steppe. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-turkic-khaganate">Second Turkic Khaganate</a> under Qapaghan extended its influence as far as the Syr Darya in the early eighth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/syr-darya.jpg" alt="Syr Darya" width="320" height="170" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Petar Milošević · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Syr_Darya.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 710, Göktürk armies led by Tonyukuk and Kül Tigin crushed the Yenisei Kyrgyz and killed their qaghan, and campaigns soon carried Göktürk influence from Manchuria to the <b>Syr Darya</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gokturk-empire">Gokturk Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Most of the Uzbek nomads later followed Abu'l-Khayr's grandson Muhammad Shaybani south into Transoxiana, and the Kazakhs became masters of most of the Dasht-i Qipchaq, the great steppe north of the <b>Syr Darya</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The empire reached its zenith under Ala ad-Din Muhammad II (r. 1200–1220), who overthrew the Ghurids in Afghanistan and the Qara Khitai in Transoxiana, creating a vast realm stretching from western Iran to the <b>Syr Darya</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…BCE distinguish several groups: the Saka Tigraxauda, 'with pointed caps', probably east of the Caspian and along the <b>Syr Darya</b>; the Saka Haumavarga, probably in Fergana and the Pamirs, whose name is usually linked to the ritual drink hauma; and the Saka Paradraya, 'beyond the sea', the Scythians…</blockquote><a href="/empire/saka">Saka · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty took its name from Seljuk Beg, a chieftain of the Qiniq clan of the Oghuz who, traditionally around 985 CE, converted to Sunni Islam and led his followers to the lower <b>Syr Darya</b> near Jand, on the frontiers of the Samanid and later Ghaznavid spheres.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The land between the Amu Darya and <b>Syr Darya</b> was settled by Scythian nomads, and its cities, Bukhara and Samarkand, grew rich as Sogdian merchants carried the silk trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
